    "body": "## What\n`_compute_indexer_scores_chunk_with_global_rows` computed indexer scores with a per-head fp32 PyTorch loop: `bmm`, then `relu_`, `mul_` and `add_` for each indexer head, all on SIMT rather than tensor cores. It serves two callers:\n- `_indexer_topk_from_score_chunks`, whenever a chunk doesn't cover every row or carries explicit lengths: long causal calls that need chunking, and calls with varlen bounds;\n- the full-score varlen branch of `_indexer_topk_bshd`.\n\nThis PR computes the scores with `indexer_forward_wrapper` instead, under the same name, and deletes the loop:\n- **Plain causal chunks:** pass `q_causal_offsets=row_start`, which is the reference mask (`(row_start + i + 1) // ratio`).\n- **Chunks with explicit lengths:** run with the tightest causal offset that covers every row, computed on device with no host sync. The exact per-row prefix is then applied, so masked positions match the reference exactly.\n\n`q_causal_offsets` is available in the pinned `nvidia-cudnn-frontend==1.29.0`.\n\n## Numerics\n- **New test** `test_dsa_cudnn_indexer_scores.py`: compares against a direct einsum reference over 9 cases: causal at ratio 1 and 4, unaligned row offsets, pooled bounds, empty rows, non-monotonic lengths, lengths beyond `sk`, packed resets.\n- **Result:** the `-inf` mask is identical in every case; max abs diff ≤ 1.2e-4 on scores up to ~300.\n- **Environment:** this branch on GB300 with `nvidia-cudnn-frontend==1.29.0`, 9/9 passing.\n\n## Performance\nMeasured on a downstream fork with a model whose indexer always passes explicit bounds (a 4:1 pooled-key indexer). 262k tokens on 8x GB300, CP4 EP8:\n- Forward/backward step: 50.7 s → 32.7 s. The fp32 loop was about 15 s of it.\n- Over 6 steps, loss and grad norm match the reference path within run-to-run noise.\n\nUpstream models gain wherever they hit chunked scoring with explicit bounds, long causal calls, or the full-score varlen branch; I haven't benchmarked an upstream model.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
